No, it is not over. Stopping the encryption ended the most visible damage, but it does not prove the attacker is gone. Investigators have found that the attacker reached beyond individual computers and obtained access that could let them return even after ordinary password changes. Resetting passwords alone would leave that route open. We do not yet know every system they touched, so our certainty is high on what has been confirmed and low on what is still unknown.

For the coming week, expect payment operations to be disrupted. Some systems must stay disconnected while we rebuild them from verified clean sources. Services will return in stages, and each system will be checked before it reconnects. Staff will be needed beyond normal hours, and we will communicate with customers and regulators as the facts are confirmed. Normal operations should not be assumed before those checks are complete.

The first decision for the board is to fund a full removal effort, covering recovery from clean sources, replacement of central access credentials, and verification of our core login servers. This is what makes the rebuild safe, and it is what stops the attacker from returning.
